-- title:
--   Integrality of Y^{2N}jmatĥ^{ mN+1}(jmatĥ-1728)^{mN} over ℂ[jmatĥ⁻¹]
-- statement:
--   Let $N \geq 1$ and $m \geq 0$ be natural numbers, and let $f$ be a cusp form of weight $2m$ (as an integer) for the congruence subgroup $\Gamma_0(N)$. Write $\hat\jmath :=$ `coeffEmb ℂ jq` for the image in $\mathbb C((q))$, under the coefficientwise extension of $\mathbb{Q} \to \mathbb{C}$, of the Laurent series `jq` $= q^{-1}\cdot$ (the rational power series obtained from the integral series `jNum` by base change), i.e. the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$; and let $\theta$ denote the $\mathbb C$-linear operator `thetaL ℂ` on $\mathbb C((q))$ sending $g$ to $q \cdot g'$, multiplication by the monomial $q$ composed with the derivative of Laurent series, so $\theta = q\,d/dq$. Let $Y \in \mathbb C((q))$ satisfy $Y \cdot (\theta\hat\jmath)^m = \hat f$, where $\hat f$ is the $q$-expansion of $f$ of width $1$, regarded as a Laurent series. The conclusion is that the element $$Y^{2N}\,\hat\jmath^{\,mN+1}\,(\hat\jmath - 1728)^{mN} \in \mathbb C((q))$$ is integral over the $\mathbb C$-subalgebra of $\mathbb C((q))$ generated by the single element $\hat\jmath^{-1}$, the inverse being taken in the field $\mathbb C((q))$ and $1728$ denoting the corresponding constant Laurent series.
--
--   This is the integrality statement underlying the passage from a weight-$2m$ cusp form on $\Gamma_0(N)$ to an algebraic relation over $\mathbb C[\hat\jmath^{-1}]$ for the associated element $Y$ of $\mathbb C((q))$, the classical mechanism being that $\theta\hat\jmath = -\hat E_4^2\hat E_6/\hat\Delta$, $\hat\jmath = \hat E_4^3/\hat\Delta$ and $\hat\jmath - 1728 = \hat E_6^2/\hat\Delta$ convert the displayed element into a quotient of $q$-expansions of modular forms. It is used in the construction of mod $p$ cusp forms from $q$-expansion data, in [`ModPForms.exists_isModPCuspFormFn_qexpOfWeight_eq_of_mem_modPCusp`](thm.html#ModPForms.exists_isModPCuspFormFn_qexpOfWeight_eq_of_mem_modPCusp).
